  /*
    Every droid crafting phase carries the same consequence.
    Ch17 does not print failure text per phase the way Ch11-Ch14 do - it
    routes all droid construction, installation and repair failures through
    one Droid Modification Malfunction Table, so each phase below repeats it
    rather than having a rule of its own.
  */
  /*
    Every droid crafting phase carries the same consequence.
    Ch17 does not print failure text per phase the way Ch11-Ch14 do - it
    routes all droid construction, installation and repair failures through
    one Droid Modification Malfunction Table, so each phase below repeats it
    rather than having a rule of its own.
  */
  /*
        CH17'S PHASE CHAIN, and the multiplier each family therefore earns.
  
        The Pricing Note and the Crafting section are ONE rule stated two ways -
        one by phase, one by part-type - and they were read as competing skill
        assignments for a whole turn. They are not:
  
          "shaping raw material into a part"   = Phase 1 = "smelt, shape, assemble"
          "wiring and fitting that part"       = Phase 2 = "wire the optic relays"
  
        Everything else the chapter says about Engineer (Droids), Mechanic or
        Armoury sits at the crippling, salvage, uninstall and repair entries, and
        those are a different OPERATION. Filtering by operation rather than by
        skill name collapses the apparent conflict to nothing.
  
        PHASE 1 IS A GENUINE FORK - Mechanic (Droids) or Armoury (Cybernetics) -
        and it is the first in this corpus whose branches share a difficulty tier
        BY DESIGN rather than by coincidence: both are IQ/Average, so the choice
        never moves the price. That is why it is spelled with fallbackPenalty 0
        (Ch10's fork) rather than as a substitute, and why check:droid-crafting
        pins the two branches to identical pricing.
  
          structural       Phase 1 x2 + Phase 2 x2                        = x4
          processor / mem  + Phase 3 Computer Programming x3              = x12
          plating, forged  Phase 1 + Phase 2 (Durasteel in the recipe)    = x4
          plating, bolt-on Phase 1 alone                                  = x2
  
        Sensors and reflex wiring look like they should take Phase 3 and do not:
        their firmware is burned in at the factory and the crafter never flashes
        it. Every one of these families used to carry a Computer Programming
        phase, which put nine of them at x6 - a multiplier the chapter's own 97
        prices contradict, and 77 mispriced blueprint rows.
      */
